dict(id="b1-exchange", level="B1", title="The Language Exchange", tr="Dil Değişimi", text="""
When Burak moved to Madrid for his {master's degree}, he could only say a few words in Spanish. He had studied the language online for six months, but real conversations were much harder than the exercises in his app. People spoke so quickly that he often felt completely lost.

One day, a classmate told him about a language exchange that {took place} every Wednesday evening in a café near the university. The idea was simple: people sat in pairs and spoke for thirty minutes in one language, then thirty minutes in the other.

At first, Burak was nervous. His partner was a woman called Lucía, who worked as a nurse and wanted to improve her English before moving to Canada. Burak was worried that his Spanish was too basic, but Lucía was patient and encouraging. When he made a mistake, she didn't interrupt him. Instead, she wrote the correct form on a napkin and showed it to him later.

After a few weeks, something changed. Burak stopped translating every sentence in his head before he spoke. He began to understand jokes, and he even made a few of his own. His confidence grew, and so did his vocabulary.

By the end of the year, Burak and Lucía had become close friends. When she finally left for Toronto, she gave him the pile of napkins she had kept. "These are all your mistakes," she said, smiling. "Look how far you've come."

Burak still has the napkins. For him, they are proof that the best way to learn a language is not to be afraid of {getting it wrong}.
"""),
dict(id="b1-phone", level="B1", title="A Phone-Free Week", tr="Telefonsuz Bir Hafta", text="""
Last spring, my company organised an unusual challenge: for one week, we had to live without our smartphones. We could use a basic phone for calls and texts, but there were no apps, no {social media} and no internet {on the go}. I agreed to {take part}, although I honestly expected to fail by Tuesday.

The first day was difficult. I {reached for} my pocket at least fifty times, out of pure habit. On the bus, I didn't know what to do with my hands, so I {looked out of} the window. I noticed shops and buildings I had never seen before, {even though} I had taken the same route for three years.

By the third day, I started to notice other changes. I {fell asleep} more easily because I wasn't scrolling through videos in bed. I also read a whole novel, something I hadn't done since university. Conversations with my colleagues at lunch felt longer and more relaxed, since nobody was checking notifications.

{Of course}, there were problems too. I {got lost} twice without a map, and I missed a message about a meeting that had been moved. Life without a smartphone is not always practical.

When the week ended, I didn't {throw away} my phone. However, I made a few rules for myself. I {turned off} most notifications, I leave my phone in the kitchen at night, and I never use it during meals. These small changes have made a big difference. I feel calmer, and I am sure I am more productive at work.

The experiment taught me that technology is a useful tool, but it shouldn't control my attention.
"""),
dict(id="b1-garden", level="B1", title="The Community Garden", tr="Mahalle Bahçesi", text="""
For years, the empty land behind Oak Street was a problem. People dumped old furniture there, and at night it felt unsafe. Most residents simply avoided it.

Then, three years ago, a retired teacher named Margaret had an idea. She {knocked on} her neighbours' doors and asked a simple question: "{What if} we turned this place into a garden?"

Many people were doubtful at first. The land was full of rubbish, and nobody knew who owned it. But Margaret was determined. She contacted the local council, which agreed to let residents use the land for free, {as long as} they {looked after} it.

On the first weekend, only seven volunteers came. They cleared the rubbish, removed broken glass and built simple wooden boxes for planting. Gradually, more neighbours joined. Some brought seeds and tools, while others offered their time and energy. A local shop donated a water tank to collect rainwater.

Today, the garden produces tomatoes, beans, herbs and strawberries. Families who live in small flats without balconies now have a place to grow their own food. Children learn where vegetables come from, and older residents, who {used to} feel lonely, have found new friends.

The garden has also changed the street itself. People stop to chat, and there are fewer problems at night. "It's not really about vegetables," Margaret says. "It's about people. The garden gave us a reason to talk to {each other}."
"""),
dict(id="b1-interview", level="B1", title="The Job Interview", tr="İş Görüşmesi", text="""
Aylin had {applied for} more than twenty jobs since graduating, but she had only been invited to two interviews, and both had gone badly. In the first one, she was so nervous that she forgot the name of the company. In the second, she couldn't answer a simple question about her weaknesses.

This time, she was determined to be better prepared. The position was at an environmental organisation, which was exactly the kind of work she wanted to do. She spent the whole weekend researching the organisation, reading its reports and making notes about its recent projects.

Her flatmate, Defne, offered to help her practise. She pretended to be the interviewer and asked difficult questions. Aylin recorded her answers on her phone and listened to them afterwards. She realised that she spoke too fast when she was anxious, so she practised {slowing down} and {taking a breath} before answering.

On the day of the interview, Aylin arrived fifteen minutes early. There were three people on the panel. When they asked about her weaknesses, she smiled. She explained that she sometimes found it hard to {say no} to extra work, and described how she was learning to manage her time better.

At the end, one of the interviewers asked if she had any questions. Aylin asked about a project she had read about, and the interviewers looked impressed.

Two days later, she received an email. She had got the job. Aylin read it three times before she believed it. Preparation, she realised, had {made all the difference}.
"""),
dict(id="b1-bus", level="B1", title="The Night Bus", tr="Gece Otobüsü", text="""
Every night at {half past} eleven, the number 47 bus leaves the city centre and travels slowly through the empty streets towards the hospital. Most passengers are tired: nurses finishing their shifts, cleaners going to work, students who have {stayed out} too late.

Hasan has been the driver of the 47 for nine years. He knows many of his regular passengers by name. There is Rosa, a cleaner who always sits at the front and tells him about her grandchildren. There is a young doctor who {falls asleep} immediately and needs to be {woken up} at his stop. And there is an old man who rides the bus from one end of the route to the other, simply because he cannot sleep and does not want to be alone.

One winter night, the bus got stuck in heavy snow. The engine was fine, but the road ahead was blocked, and the passengers had to wait for a snowplough. At first, people complained. Then Rosa opened her bag and {took out} a flask of hot tea and a box of biscuits, and she shared them with everyone. The young doctor told funny stories about his first week at the hospital. Even the old man started to talk.

They waited for almost two hours. When the road finally opened, several passengers said it had been the best journey of their lives.

Hasan still thinks about that night. "People think a bus is just a way to get from one place to another," he says. "But sometimes it's a small community on wheels."
"""),
