dict(id="b2-fourday", level="B2", title="The Four-Day Week", tr="Dört Günlük Çalışma Haftası", text="""
When the software company Northbridge announced that its employees would work four days a week for the same salary, many people assumed it was a {publicity stunt}. Critics argued that productivity would inevitably fall and that customers would suffer. Two years later, however, the results have surprised even the company's own managers.

The idea came from the company's founder, Priya Shah, who had noticed that her staff were exhausted. {Sick days} were increasing, and several talented developers had resigned, complaining of burnout. "We were losing our best people," she explains. "I realised that working longer hours was not the same as working better."

The transition was not easy. Before the trial began, every team had to analyse how it spent its time. They discovered that a surprising number of hours were wasted in unnecessary meetings and on emails that could have been avoided. Meetings were limited to thirty minutes, and employees were encouraged to protect periods of uninterrupted concentration.

{According to} the company's own figures, output remained stable during the first six months and then rose by roughly ten percent. {Staff turnover} fell dramatically, and the number of job applications tripled. Employees reported better sleep, more time with their families and a greater sense of control over their lives.

Nevertheless, the model has its limitations. Some employees admit that the four working days are more intense, and a few miss the relaxed conversations that {used to} happen in the office. Moreover, experts {point out} that what works for a software company may not suit hospitals, schools or factories, where a physical presence is essential.

Still, the experience of Northbridge has added an important voice to a growing debate. As Shah puts it, "The question is not whether we can afford to work less. It's whether we can afford not to try."
"""),
dict(id="b2-plastic", level="B2", title="The Plastic Problem", tr="Plastik Sorunu", text="""
For generations, the coastal town of Port Ellis depended on two things: fishing and tourism. Both, it seemed, depended on a clean sea. Yet by 2020, the beaches were increasingly covered in plastic waste, much of it {washed up} by the tides from hundreds of kilometres away.

The {turning point} came when a group of {secondary school} students conducted a survey for a science project. Over three months, they collected and categorised every piece of plastic they found along a single stretch of beach. The results were alarming: there were more than eight thousand items, the majority of which were {single-use} bottles, food packaging and fragments of fishing nets.

The students presented their findings at a town meeting, and the response was immediate. Local restaurants agreed to replace plastic straws and containers with biodegradable alternatives. Fishermen, who had initially felt they were being blamed, began bringing damaged nets back to port so that they could be recycled {rather than} abandoned at sea. The town council installed water fountains to reduce the demand for bottled water.

Not everyone was enthusiastic. Some shop owners complained that the alternatives were more expensive, and a few residents dismissed the campaign as symbolic, arguing that a single town could not solve a global problem. There is some truth in this criticism: much of the waste on the beaches of Port Ellis originates far beyond its control.

However, the campaign achieved something that statistics alone could not. It changed the way people thought about their own behaviour. Visitors began to notice the effort, and the reputation of the town as a responsible destination attracted a new kind of tourist.

Five years later, the students who started the project are at university, several of them studying environmental science. Their survey is repeated every summer, and each year the numbers are slightly lower. It is a modest victory, but a meaningful one.
"""),
dict(id="b2-failure", level="B2", title="Learning to Fail", tr="Başarısız Olmayı Öğrenmek", text="""
At most schools, a mistake is something to be avoided. Students lose marks for wrong answers, and many {grow up} believing that intelligence means getting things right the first time. A {secondary school} in Leeds decided to challenge this assumption, with intriguing results.

The experiment began in the maths department. Teachers noticed that many capable students refused to attempt difficult problems, preferring to leave a question blank {rather than} risk being wrong. "They weren't {lacking in} ability," says the head of the department, Daniel Okafor. "They were {lacking in} courage."

So the teachers introduced a weekly lesson called Productive Failure. Students were given problems that were deliberately too hard for them and asked to try as many approaches as possible. Wrong answers were not penalised; instead, they were displayed on the board and discussed. The class would analyse why a particular method had failed and what it revealed about the problem.

Initially, many students found the lessons frustrating. Some complained that they were wasting time. Gradually, however, attitudes began to shift. Students became more willing to {take risks}, and their explanations of their reasoning grew more sophisticated. After a year, the exam results of the school in maths had improved noticeably, particularly among students who had previously struggled.

The approach is supported by research in educational psychology, which suggests that struggling with a problem before being taught the solution can {lead to} deeper understanding. When students eventually learn the correct method, they recognise why it works, because they have already explored the alternatives.

Critics warn that the method must be used carefully. Repeated failure without support can damage the confidence of a student, and some topics simply require clear instruction. Okafor agrees. "Failure {on its own} teaches nothing," he says. "It's what you do with it that matters."

Perhaps the most significant change has been outside the classroom. Several parents have reported that their children now talk about mistakes at home without embarrassment. For a generation that is under enormous pressure to be perfect, that may be the most valuable lesson of all.
"""),
dict(id="b2-city", level="B2", title="The City That Slowed Down", tr="Yavaşlayan Şehir", text="""
A decade ago, the historic centre of Valdora was dominated by cars. Narrow streets designed for horses and carts were filled with traffic, the air was polluted, and pedestrians had to {squeeze past} vehicles parked on the pavements. Shops in the old town were closing {one after another} as residents moved to the suburbs.

The newly elected mayor proposed a radical solution: to ban private cars from the centre almost entirely. Delivery vans would be allowed in the early morning, and emergency vehicles and residents with disabilities would retain access, but everyone else would have to walk, cycle or use {public transport}.

The proposal provoked fierce opposition. Shopkeepers feared that customers would stop coming, and drivers accused the council of attacking their freedom. Local newspapers predicted that the city centre would become a {ghost town}.

What happened was quite the opposite. Once the streets were free of traffic, people began to use them differently. Children played outside, elderly residents sat on benches, and cafés extended their terraces into spaces that had previously been {car parks}. Footfall in the centre increased, and many of the shops that had struggled began to recover. Air pollution fell sharply, and serious road accidents became extremely rare.

The changes were not without costs. Some residents on the edges of the restricted zone complained that traffic had simply been pushed into their neighbourhoods. Others, particularly those living in villages with poor bus connections, found it harder to reach the centre. The council responded by building large {car parks} on the outskirts and increasing the frequency of buses, although these measures took time to have an effect.

Today, the policy enjoys broad support, and urban planners from other countries regularly visit Valdora to study its transformation. Its success suggests that the way a city is designed shapes the way its people live. When streets are built for cars, people drive. When they are built for people, life returns to them.
"""),
dict(id="b2-memory", level="B2", title="The Memory Palace", tr="Hafıza Sarayı", text="""
When Clara Jensen entered her first memory competition, she could barely remember her own {shopping list}. Five years later, she can memorise the order of a shuffled pack of {playing cards} in under a minute. She insists, however, that she was not born with an extraordinary memory. Instead, she learned a technique that is more than two thousand years old.

The method, {known as} the memory palace, is often attributed to the ancient Greek poet Simonides. According to legend, he survived the collapse of a banquet hall and was able to identify the victims by recalling where each guest had been sitting. He realised that people remember places remarkably well, and that this ability could be used to store other information.

To build a memory palace, you choose a place you know intimately, {such as} your childhood home. You then imagine walking through it along a fixed route. At each point on the route, you place a vivid, preferably absurd, image representing the item you want to remember. To recall the information, you simply walk through the palace again in your mind.

Research suggests that the technique is genuinely effective. In one {well-known} study, ordinary volunteers who practised it for six weeks more than doubled their ability to remember lists of words, and the improvement lasted for months. Brain scans also revealed that their patterns of activity had become more similar to those of memory champions.

Clara, who now teaches the technique to students, is careful not to exaggerate its value. "It's brilliant for lists, names and vocabulary," she says, "but it isn't a substitute for understanding." Memorising the dates of historical events, {for instance}, is not the same as understanding why those events happened.

Nevertheless, she believes the method offers a broader lesson. Memory is not a fixed talent that some people have and others lack; it is a skill that can be trained. "Most people think they have a bad memory," she says. "In reality, they have just never been taught how to use it."
"""),
